WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
united's  against racism on the pitch statements  against racism have now become  commonplace  leeds and manchester united the latest  to take a knee this afternoon  but much of the abuse players suffer is  off the pitch and online  which is where the sport will now take  its protest with a four-day boycott of  social media  starting next friday enough is enough  you know players are planning matches  and  they're making mistakes and then they're  getting slaughtered that doesn't that's  not right for me  so the fact that we you know as a game  and the premier league  now said this enough's enough take them  off social media  and see what effect it has former  arsenal striker thierry henry was the  first to walk away from social media  last month  citing the toxic levels of abuse some  individual clubs have followed with  temporary boycotts of their own but next  weekend it'll be the entirety of english  football  switching off from facebook twitter and  instagram  this is where the power of football as  we've seen over the last week  when it does come together is enormously  powerful and  that one single message from the whole  is far greater than the sum of anything  that comes from the parts and on  individual clubs taking that action  when racist abuse is shouted from the  stands fans can be arrested  put in prison and given lifelong bans  but when it happens  online it can often go unpunished  users can remain anonymous by hiding  behind fake accounts which is why  football believes the social media  companies themselves  must do more to stamp it out the carabao  cup final at wembley today saw fans  return for the first time since the  pandemic began  and many here feel the problem of racism  should have been tackled much sooner i  don't know why it's been going on for so  long i know social media is a big thing  in  life nowadays but there's been so many  issues with it for so long  it's only a full day thing as well isn't  it it should be a lot longer boycotts a  big thing  and it kind of forces people to listen  even if they want to ignore it they  can't  facebook and twitter say they remove  harmful content when they see it  while instagram has introduced new  software to automatically filter abuse  but off the pitch it seems there's still  a long way to go before the beautiful  game  can live up to its name iva bennett sky  news  wembley

